# .loom/resync-ignore — paths Loom must not overwrite or delete.
#
# Format: one `.loom/`-relative path per line. Blank lines and `#` comments are
# ignored; matching is exact (no globs). See `.loom/docs/repo-owned-files.md`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hooks/guard-destructive-generic.sh
#   — repo#441 (ports repo#439) + repo#545 (ports repo#436/#437)
#
# WHY THIS IS PINNED
#
# TWO ports now live in this file, both closing the same class of escape: a
# write into the main checkout that the shell really performs because a command
# substitution is EXECUTED whatever quoting wraps it. Each was fixed in the
# CANONICAL guard (`hooks/repo/guard-destructive.sh`) first and did not arrive
# here, and the vendored copy is the one a Loom-managed repo runs BEFORE the
# capability probe flips it to the canonical copy — so both escapes were live
# here.
#
# (a) repo#439 / PR #442 — a quoted SINGLE-command substitution, ported by
#     repo#441 (`bs_escaped()` / `subst_depth()` / `subst_heads()`, wired into
#     `_extract_write_targets_scan()`):
#
#     echo "$(id > <main-checkout>/evil.sh)"     # shell really performs it
#     echo "$(cp /tmp/s <main-checkout>/evil.sh)"
#     echo "`id > <main-checkout>/evil.sh`"
#
# (b) repo#436/#437 — a SEPARATOR INSIDE the substitution, and a substitution
#     inside the write TARGET, ported by repo#545 (`subst_inner()` plus an
#     opt-in `subst_depth()`-aware mode of `qsplit()`, `qsplit(buf, 1)`, called
#     from the same one place). subst_heads() could not reach these by
#     construction — a head ends at the first separator at its own depth, so
#     the write idiom after the `&&`/`|` is not in the head:
#
#     echo hi > "<main-checkout>/o-$(echo x|tr -d x).json"
#     echo "$(id|tee <main-checkout>/evil.sh)"
#     echo "$(id && cp /tmp/s <main-checkout>/e.sh)"
#
#     The mode is opt-in rather than qsplit()'s unconditional behaviour because
#     this copy's `parse_force_ops()` carries a vendored-only recognizer (#9312,
#     `NAME="$(cd <literal-path> && pwd)"`) that is BUILT ON the legacy split at
#     that inner `&&` and has no canonical counterpart. See the `sdaware`
#     paragraphs in the file's `_QSPLIT_AWK` header.
#
# `.loom/docs/repo-owned-files.md` gives exactly two durable dispositions for
# a bug in an installed Loom file: **upstream it** to `rjwalters/loom`'s
# `defaults/` tree, or **pin it** here. Upstreaming was attempted first and is
# not reachable from this repo's automation:
#
#     gh repo view rjwalters/loom --json viewerPermission  ->  ""  (no access)
#     gh api user                                          ->  403 Resource not
#                                                              accessible by
#                                                              integration
#
# This repo's `gh` credential is a GitHub App INSTALLATION token (`ghs_…`)
# scoped to this repository. It has no user context, so it can neither fork
# `rjwalters/loom` nor open a cross-repo PR from a fork. That leaves the pin
# as the only disposition an automated Builder can actually complete, and the
# fix is a hard-deny write-confinement escape that should not wait on a human
# forking round-trip. The same credential check was re-run for repo#545 with
# the same result, so (b) took the same disposition rather than a new pin.
#
# COST OF THIS PIN, STATED PLAINLY
#
# The file is now repo-owned: `resync-installed.sh` and the installer's clean
# sweep will never refresh it from upstream again, so upstream fixes to the
# generic guard stop arriving here automatically.
#
# HOW TO RETIRE IT
#
# One upstream PR retires BOTH ports at once — do not split them; (b) builds on
# (a)'s `subst_depth()` and is meaningless without it.
#
# 1. Open a PR against `rjwalters/loom`'s
#    `defaults/hooks/guard-destructive-generic.sh` carrying both ports:
#      * (a) `bs_escaped()` / `subst_depth()` / `subst_heads()` and the
#        `_extract_write_targets_scan()` wiring — search this file for
#        `repo#439`;
#      * (b) `subst_inner()`, qsplit()'s `sdaware` parameter and the three
#        `sdaware`-gated branches inside it, and the `qsplit(buf, 1)` call —
#        search this file for `repo#545`.
#    Carry the `sdaware` rationale upstream too: `defaults/`' own copy is the
#    one that will grow more `qsplit()` consumers, so the reason the mode is
#    opt-in must travel with the code.
# 2. Once it merges and a `chore: resync installed Loom surfaces` commit lands
#    a vendored copy that already denies all six shapes above, DELETE this
#    stanza and let the file resync normally again.
# 3. `commands/repo/tests/test-guard-equivalence.sh` is the regression net for
#    step 2: if a resync ever reverts either port, the six `wt:` corpus rows
#    (the repo#438 and repo#439 blocks of `guard-equivalence-cases.txt`) come
#    back as UNDECLARED stricter divergences and say so by name.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
hooks/guard-destructive-generic.sh
